{"prompt": "My flight got cancelled and I have a meeting in Berlin tomorrow morning. What do I do?", "response": "Let's fix this step by step. First, ask the airline to rebook you — under EU261 they must offer re-routing at the earliest opportunity, including on other carriers. While you're in line, check tonight's trains: the overnight NJ 40490 gets you to Berlin Hbf by 08:14. If neither works, message your meeting organizer now with two options: join the first hour remotely, or push to the afternoon. Want me to draft that message?"}
{"prompt": "How do I stop my sourdough from going flat when I bake it?", "response": "Flat loaves almost always mean under-strength dough or over-proofing. Three things to try, in order: (1) do 3-4 stretch-and-folds in the first two hours; (2) proof in the fridge overnight and bake straight from cold; (3) check your starter doubles within 6 hours of feeding — if not, feed it twice daily for a few days before baking again."}
{"prompt": "I need to explain to my boss why the server went down last night, but I only have a 5-minute window and she is not technical.", "response": "Here is a template you can adapt: \"Last night at 2:14 AM our monitoring detected that the database was using all available memory, which caused the application to stop responding. The fix was to restart the service — a standard procedure that takes less than a minute. It is back to normal and the monitoring alerts have been tuned so we are notified earlier next time.\" If she asks follow-ups, the one-liner is: the system ran out of memory and we rebooted it. No data was lost."}
{"prompt": "My cat has been vomiting every morning for a week. Should I be worried?", "response": "Yes, weekly morning vomiting warrants a vet visit — it could be anything from hairballs to a urinary issue or an intestinal foreign body. In the meantime, try feeding a smaller dinner earlier in the evening (around 5–6 PM) so the stomach is not empty by morning, which can cause bile-related nausea. Take a photo of any vomit you find and note any changes in appetite or litter-box habits to share with the vet."}
{"prompt": "Can you help me rewrite this email to sound more professional? \"Hey, just checking in on that thing we talked about last week. No rush though!\"", "response": "Here are two options, depending on how formal you want to be: Option 1 — polite but concise: \"Hi [Name], I wanted to follow up on [topic] we discussed last week. Please let me know if there is any update or if you need anything from my side.\" Option 2 — slightly warmer: \"Hi [Name], hope you are having a good week. I am circling back on [topic] from our conversation last week — happy to discuss further whenever you have a moment.\" Choose the one that best matches your relationship with the recipient."}
{"prompt": "I have two cups of flour, one cup of sugar, and some eggs. What can I bake?", "response": "With those basics you can make a simple sponge cake: beat 3 eggs with the sugar until pale and thick (about 4 minutes with a hand mixer), gently fold in the 2 cups flour, then pour into a greased pan and bake at 350F for 25-30 minutes. If you happen to have butter or milk, adding half a cup melted butter and a quarter cup milk will make it richer."}
{"prompt": "I want to start learning guitar but I get frustrated quickly. Any tips?", "response": "Three things that make a big difference for beginners: (1) Set a 10-minute daily timer instead of aiming for long sessions — consistency beats duration. (2) Start with power chords (two-finger shapes) rather than full open chords; they sound good on day one and build confidence. (3) Keep a notebook of the 2-3 songs you are learning and play the same ones for at least two weeks before moving on — your brain needs repetition more than variety at this stage. A cheap metronome app on your phone will also help you build timing from the start."}
